  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nToday: Windy and Warm\n\nStrong southwest flow is currently across the Upper Midwest this \nafternoon associated with a deepening trough over the northern \nplains. As a result the pressure gradient remains tightened over the \narea causing southwest winds to be quite breezy with sustained winds \nof 15 to 25 mph with gusts of 30 to 40 mph. The highest gusts remain \nwest of the Mississippi River. High temperatures for this afternoon \ntop out in the low to mid 60s.\n\nSaturday - Sunday: Rain\n\nAs the trough and surface low shift off to the northeast, a trailing \ncold front drops down into the Upper Midwest. With the continued \nsouthwest flow out ahead, moisture transport increases into the \nregion. Similar to the last couple of days, the NAEFS PWAT \npercentiles continue to show values between 90 and 99 indicating \nthat the forecasted PWATs are on the higher side of climatological \nnormal. The corresponding PWATs are between 0.9 to 1.1\". Due to this \nslight increase, rainfall amounts have come up a little. Current \nforecasted amounts are between 0.5 to 1\" within the areas that see \nrain for the longest duration. Similar to the last few forecasts, \nthere has been an increase in the probability for at least 1\" to \noccur, between 30 and 50%. As shown by CAMs and other models, this \nwill be a very narrow corridor from central Iowa into portions of \nsouthwest Wisconsin where the highest amounts will fall. This \nboundary then shifts off to the east, indicating that the short \nduration of rain that occurs will limit any heavy rain potential. \nShifting gears to talk about temperatures, highs on Saturday \ncontinue to stay in the low to mid 60s as the frontal passage occurs \nduring the mid to late afternoon. Highs on Sunday behind the front \nremain in the upper 30s to mid 40s. With cold air advection filling \nin behind the front on Sunday, the pressure gradient tightens and \ncauses the northwest winds to be on the breezy side with sustained \nwinds of 15 to 20 mph with gusts of 25 to 30 mph. \n\nNext Week: Cooler and Mostly Dry\n\nThe upper-air pattern for next week flips to primarily zonal to \nnorthwest flow. Despite instances of shortwave troughing or ridging, \ntemperatures will remain on the cooler side with highs staying in \nthe 30s to low 40s and low temperatures ranging from the mid teens \nto mid 20s. Monday and Tuesday will be dry as a surface high shifts \noverhead. The next chance for any type of precipitation looks to be \non Wednesday when a shortwave trough passes through the region. \nEnsemble members are not too excited about this system as only \naround 10 to 20% of them show measurable precipitation.",
